1 Introduction
---------------


![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x1.png)
Figure 1: RvS learning conditioned on goals (RvS-G) and on rewards (RvS-R) compared with prior approaches and baselines. Each bar is the average over many tasks in each suite. Using just supervised learning with a feedforward MLP, RvS matches the performance of methods employing TD learning and Transformer sequence models. <https://github.com/scottemmons/rvs>


Offline and off-policy reinforcement learning (RL) are typically addressed using value-based methods. While theoretically appealing because they include performance guarantees under certain assumptions [[27](#bib.bib14 "Conservative Q-learning for offline reinforcement learning")], such methods can be difficult to apply in practice; they tend to require complex tricks to stabilize learning and delicate tuning of many hyperparameters.
Recent work has explored an alternative approach: convert the RL problem into a *conditional*, *filtered*, or *weighted* imitation learning problem.
This typically uses a simple insight: suboptimal experience for one task may be optimal for another task. By conditioning on some piece of information, such as a goal, reward function parameterization, or reward value, such experience can be used for simple behavior cloning [[6](#bib.bib7 "End-to-end driving via conditional imitation learning"), [29](#bib.bib23 "Learning latent plans from play"), [8](#bib.bib22 "Goal-conditioned imitation learning"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning"), [17](#bib.bib13 "Learning actionable representations with goal conditioned policies"), [34](#bib.bib39 "Advantage-weighted regression: simple and scalable off-policy reinforcement learning"), [32](#bib.bib40 "Fitted Q-iteration by advantage weighted regression"), [26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [9](#bib.bib29 "Rewriting history with inverse RL: hindsight inference for policy improvement"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling"), [23](#bib.bib49 "Offline reinforcement learning as one big sequence modeling problem")]. We refer to this set of approaches as RL via supervised learning (RvS). These approaches commonly condition on goals [[29](#bib.bib23 "Learning latent plans from play"), [6](#bib.bib7 "End-to-end driving via conditional imitation learning"), [18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")] or reward values [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")], but they can also involve reweighting or filtering [[9](#bib.bib29 "Rewriting history with inverse RL: hindsight inference for policy improvement"), [32](#bib.bib40 "Fitted Q-iteration by advantage weighted regression"), [34](#bib.bib39 "Advantage-weighted regression: simple and scalable off-policy reinforcement learning"), [26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies")].




RvS methods are appealing because of their algorithmic simplicity. However, prior work has put forward conflicting hypotheses about which factors are essential for their good performance, including online data [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")], advantage weighting [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies")], or large Transformer sequence models [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")].
The first question we study is: what elements are essential for effective RvS learning?
Beyond this, it also remains unclear on which tasks and datasets such methods work well. For example, prior work has argued that temporal compositionality (dubbed “subtrajectory stitching”) is an important component for solving offline RL when there are few near-optimal trajectories present in the data (e.g., the Franka Kitchen and AntMaze tasks in D4RL [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")]). A priori, one might expect that dynamic programming via TD learning is needed for these tasks. So we also ask: what are the limits of RvS learning, and does it scale to settings with few near-optimal trajectories?





The main contribution of this paper is a study of RvS methods on a wide range of offline RL problems, as well as a set of analyses about which design factors matter most for such methods.
First, we show that pure supervised learning (maximizing the likelihood of actions observed in the data) performs as well as conservative TD learning in every task we consider. Second, simple feedforward models can match the performance of more complex sequence models from prior work across a wide range of tasks. Finally, choosing to condition on reward values versus goals can have a large effect on performance, with different choices working better in different domains.
These simple results contradict the narrative put forward in many prior works that argue for more complex design decisions [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [27](#bib.bib14 "Conservative Q-learning for offline reinforcement learning"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")]. To the best of our knowledge, our results match or exceed those reported by any prior RvS method. We believe that these findings will be useful to the RL community because they help to understand the essential ingredients and limitations of RvS methods, providing a foundation for future work on simple and performant offline RL algorithms.

2 Related Work
---------------



Recent offline RL methods use many algorithmic techniques, including value functions [[27](#bib.bib14 "Conservative Q-learning for offline reinforcement learning"), [15](#bib.bib11 "Off-policy deep reinforcement learning without exploration")], dynamics estimation alongisde value functions [[42](#bib.bib52 "MOPO: Model-based Offline Policy Optimization"), [37](#bib.bib53 "Model-based offline policy optimization with distribution correcting regularization"), [24](#bib.bib55 "MOReL: Model-Based Offline Reinforcement Learning")], dynamics estimation alone [[7](#bib.bib59 "Learning and policy search in stochastic dynamical systems with bayesian neural networks"), [3](#bib.bib54 "Model-based offline planning"), [39](#bib.bib60 "Overcoming model bias for robust offline deep reinforcement learning")], and uncertainty quantification [[1](#bib.bib56 "An optimistic perspective on offline reinforcement learning"), [41](#bib.bib57 "Uncertainty weighted actor-critic for offline reinforcement learning"), [42](#bib.bib52 "MOPO: Model-based Offline Policy Optimization")]. In this paper, we focus on offline RL methods based on conditional behavior cloning that avoid value functions.
The most common instantiation of these methods are goal-conditioned behavior cloning [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning"), [29](#bib.bib23 "Learning latent plans from play"), [8](#bib.bib22 "Goal-conditioned imitation learning")] and reward-conditioned behavior cloning [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning"), [35](#bib.bib34 "Reinforcement learning upside down: don’t predict rewards–just map them to actions")]. Prior work has also looked at conditioning on different information, such as many previous timesteps [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling"), [23](#bib.bib49 "Offline reinforcement learning as one big sequence modeling problem")], tasks inferred by inverse RL [[9](#bib.bib29 "Rewriting history with inverse RL: hindsight inference for policy improvement")], and other task information [[6](#bib.bib7 "End-to-end driving via conditional imitation learning"), [21](#bib.bib8 "Urban driving with conditional imitation learning")].
While these methods can be directly applied to the offline RL setting, some prior works combine these methods with iterative data collection [[38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning"), [18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")], which is not permitted in the typical offline RL setting.
Kumar et al. [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies")] study reward-conditioned policies and do present experiments in the offline RL setting. However, in contrast with our work, the results from Kumar et al. [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies")] suggest that good performance cannot be achieved through RvS learning but only through additional advantage-weighting [[32](#bib.bib40 "Fitted Q-iteration by advantage weighted regression"), [34](#bib.bib39 "Advantage-weighted regression: simple and scalable off-policy reinforcement learning")]. More recent work [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")] presents a conditional imitation learning method for the offline RL setting that avoids advantage weighting by introducing a higher capacity, autoregressive Transformer policy. In contrast to these prior works,
we show that simple conditioning with standard feedforward networks can attain state-of-the-art results. While these results do not require advantage weighting or high-capacity sequence models, they do require careful tuning of the policy capacity.







