While the basic formulation of this conditional policy is simple, instantiating RvS methods that attain excellent results has proven challenging [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning")]. In the remainder of this paper, we present an empirical analysis of the design choices in RvS methods. We aim to understand which design choices are needed to make RvS methods perform well on diverse benchmark tasks, including how two different choices for ω (goals and rewards) compare in practice.

4 Tasks and Datasets
---------------------



To provide a comprehensive empirical study of RvS methods, we selected a broad range of tasks; the state-based tasks in prior work on various types of RvS or conditional imitation methods typically include only a subset of the domains we consider [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")].
Our goal will be to include: *(1)* domains and datasets that are appropriate for different types of conditioning, including goals and rewards; *(2)* datasets that include different proportions of near-optimal data, ranging from near-expert datasets to ones with very few or no optimal trajectories at all; *(3)* datasets that run the gamut in terms of task dimensionality; *(4)* domains that have been studied by prior value-based offline RL methods.



![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x3.png)


GCSL is a suite of goal-conditioned environments used by Ghosh et al. [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")] to evaluate GCSL, a goal-conditioned RvS method with online data collection. We adapt these tasks for offline RL by using a *random* policy to collect training data, which results in suboptimal trajectories. The tasks include 2D navigation with obstacles (FourRooms, Eysenbach et al. [[10](#bib.bib31 "Search on the replay buffer: bridging planning and reinforcement learning")]); two end-effector controlled Sawyer robotic arm tasks (Door and Pusher, Nair et al. [[31](#bib.bib20 "Visual reinforcement learning with imagined goals")]); the Lunar Lander video game, which requires controlling thrusters to land a simulated Lunar Excursion Module (Lander); and a manipulation task that requires rotating a valve with a robotic claw (Claw, Ahn et al. [[2](#bib.bib15 "ROBEL: RObotics BEnchmarks for Learning with low-cost robots")]).




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x4.png)


Gym Locomotion v2 tasks consist of the HalfCheetah, Hopper, and Walker datasets from the D4RL offline RL benchmark [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")]. We use the random, medium, medium-expert, and medium-replay datasets in our evaluations, which consist of (a mixture of different) policies with varying levels of optimality. This requires learning from mixed and suboptimal data, and we will see that TD learning methods perform comparatively well on the random data.




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x5.png)


Franka Kitchen v0 is a 9-DoF robotic manipulation task paired with datasets of human demonstrations. This task originates from Gupta et al. [[19](#bib.bib24 "Relay policy learning: solving long-horizon tasks via imitation and reinforcement learning")] and was formalized as an offline RL task in D4RL [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")]. Solving this task requires composing multi-step behaviors (e.g., open the microwave, then flip a switch) from component skills. This task includes three datasets: complete, which solves all tasks in order; partial, where only a subset of trajectories perform the desired tasks in order; and mixed, which contains all subtasks but *never* in the right order, requiring generalization and temporal composition.




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x6.png)


AntMaze v2 involves controlling an 8-DoF quadruped to navigate to a particular goal state. This benchmark task, from D4RL [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")], uses a non-Markovian demonstrator policy and was intended to test an agent’s ability to learn temporal compositionality by combining subtrajectories of different demonstrations. There are three mazes of increasing size: umaze, medium, and large, and there are two datasets types: diverse and play. The D4RL paper [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")] claims that “diverse” data navigates from random start locations to random goal locations while “play” navigates between hand-picked start-goal pairs.
Prior work has proposed that, for tasks requiring temporal compositionality, dynamic programming with value-based methods should be particularly important [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")]. However, we find in AntMaze that RvS outperforms all the dynamic programming methods we consider.





When applying RvS learning methods, one must choose a type of outcome, such as rewards or goals.
In Kitchen and AntMaze, the reward function is implemented in terms of goals. For kitchen, the reward is to complete subtasks in order, so we condition RvS-G on a state in which all the subtasks have been achieved. For AntMaze, we condition RvS-G on the goal location in the maze. This choice makes the additional assumption that we know how the reward function is defined, rather than just receiving samples from the reward function. Because GCSL is inherently a multitask, goal-conditioned setup, we omit reward conditioning and only report RvS-G. Conversely, because there is no clear way to define performance in Gym w.r.t. a goal, we omit goal conditioning and only report RvS-R.
For all tasks, we report scores in the range [0,100]. For GCSL, the score corresponds to the percentage of episodes in which the agent achieves the goal. In the other environments, we follow Fu et al. [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")] and normalize the score according to 100⋅return−randomexpert−random. (Images from [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning"), [11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")].)




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x7.png)
Figure 3: RvS Architecture: We concatenate the current state with a future outcome, which is a goal state (RvS-G) or a reward target (RvS-R). Then, we predict actions with an MLP.

5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization
---------------------------------------------



We instantiate the RvS framework in Section [3](#S3 "3 Reinforcement Learning via Supervised Learning ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") using a simple feedforward, fully-connected neural network. We condition on either the reward-to-go [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning")] or a goal state [[29](#bib.bib23 "Learning latent plans from play"), [19](#bib.bib24 "Relay policy learning: solving long-horizon tasks via imitation and reinforcement learning"), [18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")] by merely concatenating with the input state (see Figure [3](#S4.F3 "Figure 3 ‣ 4 Tasks and Datasets ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?")). Our aim is to identify the *essential* components of such methods; we eschew more complex architectures such as Transformer sequence models [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")].
On the tasks we consider, we show that our simple implementation achieves performance competitive with prior work that uses these more complex components. However, seemingly minor choices in the architecture do matter. These architectural choices include tuning capacity and regularization, suggesting that overfitting and underfitting are major challenges for RvS. Additionally, the choice of what to condition on (goals or rewards) also has important and domain-specific repercussions.




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/figures/network-architecture-catplot_v4.png)
Figure 4: Capacity and regularization:
We vary capacity (via network width) and regularization (via dropout) on one environment from each task suite. Larger networks perform better on hopper-medium-expert and kitchen-complete, suggesting the importance of high-capacity policy networks. However, dropout also usually boosts performance in kitchen-complete, suggesting that a combination of high-capacity policies with effective regularization is important for achieving good results.



### Capacity and regularization.



In Figure [4](#S5.F4 "Figure 4 ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?"), we compare different architecture sizes and regularization settings on a single task from each task suite. The best-performing architectures are generally larger than the architectures used in standard online RL [[28](#bib.bib5 "Continuous control with deep reinforcement learning.")] and imitation learning [[22](#bib.bib3 "Generative adversarial imitation learning"), [12](#bib.bib6 "Learning robust rewards with adverserial inverse reinforcement learning")]. This conclusion is intuitive, since RvS policies must represent both the optimal policy *and policies for other conditioning values* (e.g., other goals or suboptimal rewards). This result may help explain the contradictory conclusions from prior work regarding the importance of more complex design decisions [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")].
The results in Figure [4](#S5.F4 "Figure 4 ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") also highlight the importance of regularization: while dropout sometimes has no effect (pusher), it improves performance in some tasks (kitchen-complete) and worsens performance in other tasks (hopper-medium-expert and antmaze-medium-play).




What can we say about these results on network capacity and regularization? The hopper-medium-expert dataset is large, and it contains only two modes: one from a medium-quality policy, and one from an expert policy. So, it is not surprising that this data doesn’t need regularization. In contrast, kitchen-complete is a small dataset of human demonstrations solving many different subtasks. Prior work has found that human demonstrations are harder to fit than artificial demonstrations [[30](#bib.bib66 "What matters in learning from offline human demonstrations for robot manipulation")], and this may explain why kitchen-complete generally benefits from dropout. In antmaze-medium-play, relatively few trajectories are successful, so we are surprised to see that the best-performing hyperparameter setting does not use regularization. In pusher, regularization neither helps nor hurts, underscoring that the (lack of) impact of regularization depends on the task at hand.
Overall, we speculate that regularization balances a tension between two competing demands on the policy. First, even if the behavior policy is simple, the *conditional* action distribution may be complex. Second, the policy must be sufficiently well regularized to generalize well to new goals or conditioning variables.



![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/figures/gcsl_distribution_ablation.png)
Figure 5: Output distributions.



### Output distributions.



The form of the policy’s output distribution also influences the policy’s capacity.
While unimodal Gaussians are a common choice with continuous action spaces [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [14](#bib.bib4 "A minimalist approach to offline reinforcement learning"), [27](#bib.bib14 "Conservative Q-learning for offline reinforcement learning")],
a categorical distribution over a discretized action space allows the policy to represent more complex, multi-modal distributions [[29](#bib.bib23 "Learning latent plans from play"), [18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")]. To study the importance of the policy output distribution, we use the low-dimensional GCSL environments, where we can discretize the full action space.
Figure [5](#S5.F5 "Figure 5 ‣ Capacity and regularization. ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") shows that across the GCSL suite, categorical distributions either match or outperform Gaussian distributions. Similar to the results from the previous section, these results highlight how increasing the policy’s model capacity can improve performance.
Again, our finding stands in contrast to standard RL methods, which work well with unimodal Gaussians [[20](#bib.bib65 "Soft actor-critic: off-policy maximum entropy deep reinforcement learning with a stochastic actor"), [36](#bib.bib43 "Proximal policy optimization algorithms")]
We conjecture that upweighting better data, as done by previous methods [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies")], may make it easier for low-capacity policies to fit the data. While both upweighting data and increasing model capacity allow the policy to better fit the data, increasing model capacity may be a simpler approach.




![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/figures/val-tuning-regression.png)
Figure 6: Model selection using validation loss: On the kitchen task, validation loss is only loosely correlated with final performance.



### Tuning with validation loss.



Hyperparameter optimization is especially important in the offline RL setting, as evaluating different hyperparameter configurations requires interacting with the environment [[33](#bib.bib58 "Hyperparameter selection for offline reinforcement learning")]. Since RvS methods reduce the RL problem to a supervised learning problem, we conjecture that the validation loss of this supervised learning problem might be an effective metric for tuning important hyperparameters, such as policy capacity and regularization.
To test this conjcture, we train models on 80% of the training data for each Franka Kitchen dataset with two different hyperparameter settings: a regularized setting with dropout p=0.1 and small batch size 256; and an unregularized setting without dropout and a large batch size of 16,384.
We show results in Figure [6](#S5.F6 "Figure 6 ‣ Output distributions. ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?"). While for all three datasets validation set error does correlate with performance, the strength of this correlation varies significantly. In general, the validation loss does not provide a reliable approach for hyperparameter tuning. Fully automated tuning of hyperparameters remains an open question [[33](#bib.bib58 "Hyperparameter selection for offline reinforcement learning"), [13](#bib.bib61 "Benchmarks for deep off-policy evaluation")].






### A recipe for practitioners.



We suggest the following process for online hyperparameter tuning: incrementally increase network width until performance saturates, and then try adding a bit of dropout regularization (e.g., dropout p=0.1). If validation loss indicates that under/overfitting is an issue, one can also try increasing/decreasing the batch size, respectively.




Table [1](#S5.T1 "Table 1 ‣ A recipe for practitioners. ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") summarizes the hyperparameters that we found to work best for each offline RL benchmark.






