6 Comparing RvS with Prior Offline RL Methods
----------------------------------------------



Having identified key design decisions in the implementation of RvS learning methods, we now compare RvS learning methods to prior offline RL methods. We will evaluate both goal-conditioned behavioral cloning (RvS-G) and reward-conditioned behavioral cloning (RvS-R) using the domains in Section [4](#S4 "4 Tasks and Datasets ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?").
We then discuss what these results imply about the performance, design parameters, and limitations of RvS methods.





### Baselines and prior methods.



On the D4RL benchmarks, we compare RvS learning to *(i)* value-based methods and *(ii)* prior supervised learning methods and behavioral cloning baselines. For *(i)*, we include CQL [[27](#bib.bib14 "Conservative Q-learning for offline reinforcement learning")] as well as the more recent TD3+BC [[14](#bib.bib4 "A minimalist approach to offline reinforcement learning")] and Onestep RL [[4](#bib.bib2 "Offline RL without off-policy evaluation")] methods. TD3+BC and Onestep RL both involve elements of behavior cloning.
We use CQL-p to denote the CQL numbers published in [[11](#bib.bib18 "D4RL: Datasets for deep data-driven reinforcement learning")] and CQL-r to denote our best attempt to replicate these results using open-source code and hyperparameters from the CQL authors.
For *(ii)*, we include behavioral cloning (BC), which does not perform any conditioning; Filtered (“Filt.”) BC, a baseline appearing in [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")] which performs BC after filtering for the trajectories with highest cumulative reward; and Decision Transformer (DT) [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")], which conditions on rewards and uses a large Transformer sequence model.
Both BC and Filtered BC are our own optimized implementations [[25](#bib.bib67 "JAXRL: Implementations of Reinforcement Learning algorithms in JAX")], tuned thoroughly in a similar manner as RvS-R and RvS-G. In Kitchen and Gym locomotion, Filtered BC clones the top 10% of trajectories based on cumulative reward. In AntMaze, Filtered BC clones all successful trajectories (those with a reward of 1) and ignores the failing trajectories (those with a reward of 0).
On the GCSL benchmarks, we compare to GCSL using numbers reported by the authors [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")]. This gives the GCSL baseline the advantage of online data, whereas RvS uses only offline data. We do not run RvS-G on the Gym locomotion tasks, which are typically expressed as reward-maximization tasks, not goal-reaching tasks.
For additional details about the baselines, see Appendix [A](#A1 "Appendix A Experiment Details ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?").






### Overall performance and comparison to prior methods.



Figure [1](#S1.F1 "Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") show the results of this comparison, leading to several conclusions about how our RvS implementations compare to prior value-based and supervised methods.
On all tasks, either RvS-G or RvS-R attains results that are comparable to the best prior method.
The finding that conditional imitation with standard fully connected networks attains competitive results stands in contrast to prior work, which emphasizes advantage weighting and Transformer sequence models [[26](#bib.bib50 "Reward-conditioned policies"), [5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")]. As we discussed in Section [5](#S5 "5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?"), good performance requires a careful combination of high capacity and regularization, and it may simply be that this seemingly contradictory combination of components was not adequately explored in prior work.





### Subtrajectory stitching.



The mixed dataset in Kitchen and all of the AntMaze tasks consist of suboptimal trajectories. Attaining expert performance requires recombining parts of these trajectories. Such stitching is usually viewed as a key benefit of dynamic programming methods, so it is surprising that RvS-G performs as well as prior methods based on dynamic programming (TD3+BC and CQL).
In these same tasks, RvS-G outperforms RvS-R. We speculate that the spatial information encoded by goal information helps RvS-G generalize.
BC works well in the Kitchen environment but poorly in the AntMaze tasks, perhaps becaue the Kitchen task contains experience that it easier to imitate.
As a direction for future work, we propose studying if conditioning on goals can help provide a method with compositionality in space in the same way that the Bellman backup provides compositionality in time.



![](https://media.arxiv-vanity.com/render-output/7137017/x8.png)
Figure 7: GCSL results



### Reaching arbitrary goals.



An important capability of RvS-G, compared with more standard offline RL methods, is that it learns a policy that can reach many goals. To test how well RvS-G can learn to reach arbitrary goals from random offline data, we compare to GCSL, an RvS learning method that performs *iterative* online data collection [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning")]. We use the same tasks used by GCSL.
Each of the tasks in the GCSL suite requires that the agent reach a goal drawn uniformly at random from the set of possible goals.
The results shown in Figure [7](#S6.F7 "Figure 7 ‣ Subtrajectory stitching. ‣ 6 Comparing RvS with Prior Offline RL Methods ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") indicate that RvS-G can successfully reach many different goals entirely from randomly collected offline data. Although it may still be that iterative online collection, as proposed in prior work [[18](#bib.bib1 "Learning to reach goals via iterated supervised learning"), [38](#bib.bib33 "Training agents using upside-down reinforcement learning")], may be helpful in some domains, in this case simple offline training appears to suffice.





### Random datasets.



While on average RvS-R is competitive with prior methods on the Gym Locomotion tasks (Figure [1](#S1.F1 "Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?")), TD learning performs better on the random datasets; indeed, CQL performs especially well on halfcheetah-random (Table [2](#A0.T2 "Table 2 ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?")).
This suggests that RvS methods may more generally perform worse on random datasets in comparison to TD learning methods.





### Analysis of reward conditioning.



Next, we analyze what RvS-R actually learns and how it uses the conditioning variable. First, we use the walker2d-medium-expert task to analyze the behavior of RvS-R for target rewards. The walker2d-medium-expert dataset contains two modes (“medium” and “expert”, as shown in Figure [8](#S6.F8 "Figure 8 ‣ Analysis of reward conditioning. ‣ 6 Comparing RvS with Prior Offline RL Methods ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") *(left)*. Conditioning the network on a range of reward targets, Figure [8](#S6.F8 "Figure 8 ‣ Analysis of reward conditioning. ‣ 6 Comparing RvS with Prior Offline RL Methods ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?") *(right)* shows that the policy learns to achieve just the two modes in the dataset. The policy cannot interpolate between them. In effect, it appears that RvS is mimicking a subset of the demonstrations, but doing so without explicit filtering (as is done in Filtered BC).




This analysis highlights the importance of the conditioning variable. It is not obvious a priori that a reward target of 100 will lead to a return of 70, whereas a reward target of 110 will lead to a return of 105.
Following prior work [[5](#bib.bib16 "Decision transformer: reinforcement learning via sequence modeling")], we tune the reward target for every task (see Table [1](#S5.T1 "Table 1 ‣ A recipe for practitioners. ‣ 5 Architecture, Capacity, and Regularization ‣ RvS: What is Essential for Offline RL via Supervised Learning?")). Choosing this hyperparameter in a truly offline setting is an important problem for future work.






